{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-265990" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the first four terms of the binomial expansion of $(1+x)^{5}$ in ascending powers of $x$ to approximate $(1.11)^{5}.$ Round your answer to three decimal places.\n\n$(1.11)^{5}≈$ \n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060524/state.json" "file_sha256" "49caef80cec01ece4de45aa02bc0c2195026733ab72b69c196f06cc47649d776"} {"question" "q-265990" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that when $n$ is a positive integer, \n\n$$\n(1+x)^{n}=1+nx+\\frac{1}{2!}n(n-1){x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3!}n(n-1)(n-2){x}^{3}+⋯+{x}^{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(1+x)}^{5} & =1+5x+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 5\\cdot 4\\cdot {x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 5\\cdot 4\\cdot 3\\cdot {x}^{3}+⋯+{x}^{5} \\\\  & =1+5x+10{x}^{2}+10{x}^{3}+⋯\\,+{x}^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(1+x)}^{5}≈1+5x+10{x}^{2}+10{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=0.11$ into the above approximation of $(1+x)^{5}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1.11)^{5} & =(1+0.11)^{5} \\\\  & ≈1+5(0.11)+10(0.11)^{2}+10(0.11)^{3} \\\\  & =1+5(0.11)+10(0.012\\,1)+10(0.001\\,331) \\\\  & =1+0.55+0.121+0.013\\,31 \\\\  & ≈1.684,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060524/state.json" "file_sha256" "49caef80cec01ece4de45aa02bc0c2195026733ab72b69c196f06cc47649d776"} {"question" "q-265990" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060524/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "657a1d0c9fc298b0bfd76bf9bfcf42171f33a0ba2583e289c6034e93f1c4b415"} {"question" "q-265990" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060524/state.json" "file_sha256" "49caef80cec01ece4de45aa02bc0c2195026733ab72b69c196f06cc47649d776"} {"question" "q-265990" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1.684"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060524/state.json" "file_sha256" "49caef80cec01ece4de45aa02bc0c2195026733ab72b69c196f06cc47649d776"} {"question" "q-134606" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(1+x)^{7}$ in ascending powers of $x$ to approximate $(1.12)^{7}.$ Give your answer to four decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060524/state.json" "file_sha256" "49caef80cec01ece4de45aa02bc0c2195026733ab72b69c196f06cc47649d776"} {"question" "q-134606" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that when $n$ is a positive integer, \n\n$$\n(1+x)^{n}=1+nx+\\frac{1}{2!}n(n-1){x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(1+x)}^{7} & =1+7x+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 7\\cdot 6\\cdot {x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{7} \\\\  & =1+7x+21{x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(1+x)}^{7}≈1+7x+21{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=0.12$ into the above approximation of $(1+x)^{7}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1.12)^{7} & =(1+0.12)^{7} \\\\  & ≈1+7(0.12)+21(0.12)^{2} \\\\  & =1+7(0.12)+21(0.014\\,4) \\\\  & =1+0.84+0.302\\,4 \\\\  & =2.142\\,4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto four decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060524/state.json" "file_sha256" "49caef80cec01ece4de45aa02bc0c2195026733ab72b69c196f06cc47649d776"} {"question" "q-134606" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060524/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "657a1d0c9fc298b0bfd76bf9bfcf42171f33a0ba2583e289c6034e93f1c4b415"} {"question" "q-134606" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060524/state.json" "file_sha256" "49caef80cec01ece4de45aa02bc0c2195026733ab72b69c196f06cc47649d776"} {"question" "q-134606" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2.142\\,4"] ["math" "2.145\\,1"] ["math" "2.149\\,6"] ["math" "2.164\\,6"] ["math" "2.210\\,7"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060524/state.json" "file_sha256" "49caef80cec01ece4de45aa02bc0c2195026733ab72b69c196f06cc47649d776"} {"question" "q-134606" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2.142\\,4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060524/state.json" "file_sha256" "49caef80cec01ece4de45aa02bc0c2195026733ab72b69c196f06cc47649d776"} {"question" "q-265899" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the first four terms of the binomial expansion of $(1+x)^{10}$ in ascending powers of $x$ to approximate $(1.15)^{10}.$ Round your answer to four decimal places.\n\n$(1.15)^{10}≈$ \n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060524/state.json" "file_sha256" "49caef80cec01ece4de45aa02bc0c2195026733ab72b69c196f06cc47649d776"} {"question" "q-265899" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that when $n$ is a positive integer, \n\n$$\n(1+x)^{n}=1+nx+\\frac{1}{2!}n(n-1){x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3!}n(n-1)(n-2){x}^{3}+⋯+{x}^{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(1+x)}^{10} & =1+10x+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 10\\cdot 9\\cdot {x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 10\\cdot 9\\cdot 8\\cdot {x}^{3}+⋯+{x}^{10} \\\\  & =1+10x+45{x}^{2}+120{x}^{3}+⋯\\,+{x}^{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(1+x)}^{10}≈1+10x+45{x}^{2}+120{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=0.15$ into the above approximation of $(1+x)^{10}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1.15)^{10} & =(1+0.15)^{10} \\\\  & ≈1+10(0.15)+45(0.15)^{2}+120(0.15)^{3} \\\\  & =1+10(0.15)+45(0.022\\,5)+120(0.003\\,375) \\\\  & =1+1.5+1.012\\,5+0.405 \\\\  & ≈3.917\\,5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to four decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060524/state.json" "file_sha256" "49caef80cec01ece4de45aa02bc0c2195026733ab72b69c196f06cc47649d776"} {"question" "q-265899" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060524/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "657a1d0c9fc298b0bfd76bf9bfcf42171f33a0ba2583e289c6034e93f1c4b415"} {"question" "q-265899" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060524/state.json" "file_sha256" "49caef80cec01ece4de45aa02bc0c2195026733ab72b69c196f06cc47649d776"} {"question" "q-265899" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3.9175"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060524/state.json" "file_sha256" "49caef80cec01ece4de45aa02bc0c2195026733ab72b69c196f06cc47649d776"} {"question" "q-265895" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(