e" [:answer.type/math "1.403"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060524/state.json" "file_sha256" "49caef80cec01ece4de45aa02bc0c2195026733ab72b69c196f06cc47649d776"} {"question" "q-234172" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(1+x)^{5}$ in ascending powers of $x$ to approximate $(1.19)^{5}.$ Round your answer to three decimal places.\n\n$(1.19)^{5}≈$ \n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060524/state.json" "file_sha256" "49caef80cec01ece4de45aa02bc0c2195026733ab72b69c196f06cc47649d776"} {"question" "q-234172" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that when $n$ is a positive integer, \n\n$$\n(1+x)^{n}=1+nx+\\frac{1}{2!}n(n-1){x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(1+x)}^{5} & =1+5x+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 5\\cdot 4\\cdot {x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{5} \\\\  & =1+5x+10{x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(1+x)}^{5}≈1+5x+10{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=0.19$ into the above approximation of $(1+x)^{5}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1.19)^{5} & =(1+0.19)^{5} \\\\  & ≈1+5(0.19)+10(0.19)^{2} \\\\  & =1+5(0.19)+10(0.036\\,1) \\\\  & =1+0.95+0.361 \\\\  & ≈2.311,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060524/state.json" "file_sha256" "49caef80cec01ece4de45aa02bc0c2195026733ab72b69c196f06cc47649d776"} {"question" "q-234172" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060524/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "657a1d0c9fc298b0bfd76bf9bfcf42171f33a0ba2583e289c6034e93f1c4b415"} {"question" "q-234172" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060524/state.json" "file_sha256" "49caef80cec01ece4de45aa02bc0c2195026733ab72b69c196f06cc47649d776"} {"question" "q-234172" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2.311"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060524/state.json" "file_sha256" "49caef80cec01ece4de45aa02bc0c2195026733ab72b69c196f06cc47649d776"} {"question" "q-266001" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the first four terms of the binomial expansion of $(1+x)^{10}$ in ascending powers of $x$ to approximate $(0.84)^{10}.$ Round your answer to five decimal places.\n\n$(0.84)^{10}≈$ \n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060524/state.json" "file_sha256" "49caef80cec01ece4de45aa02bc0c2195026733ab72b69c196f06cc47649d776"} {"question" "q-266001" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that when $n$ is a positive integer, \n\n$$\n(1+x)^{n}=1+nx+\\frac{1}{2!}n(n-1){x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3!}n(n-1)(n-2){x}^{3}+⋯+{x}^{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(1+x)}^{10} & =1+10x+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 10\\cdot 9\\cdot {x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 10\\cdot 9\\cdot 8\\cdot {x}^{3}+⋯+{x}^{10} \\\\  & =1+10x+45{x}^{2}+120{x}^{3}+⋯\\,+{x}^{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(1+x)}^{10}≈1+10x+45{x}^{2}+120{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=-0.16$ into the above approximation of $(1+x)^{10}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0.84)^{10} & =(1+(-0.16))^{10} \\\\  & ≈1+10(-0.16)+45(-0.16)^{2}+120(-0.16)^{3} \\\\  & =1-10(0.16)+45(0.025\\,6)-120(0.004\\,096) \\\\  & =1-1.6+1.152-0.491\\,52 \\\\  & ≈0.060\\,48\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to five decimal places." "