for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(1+x)}^{5}≈1+5x+10{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=-0.06$ into the above approximation of $(1+x)^{5}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0.94)^{6} & =(1+(-0.06))^{5} \\\\  & ≈1+5(-0.06)+10(-0.06)^{2} \\\\  & =1-5(0.06)+10(0.003\\,6) \\\\  & =1-0.3+0.036 \\\\  & =0.736,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto three decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060524/state.json" "file_sha256" "49caef80cec01ece4de45aa02bc0c2195026733ab72b69c196f06cc47649d776"} {"question" "q-134609" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060524/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "657a1d0c9fc298b0bfd76bf9bfcf42171f33a0ba2583e289c6034e93f1c4b415"} {"question" "q-134609" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060524/state.json" "file_sha256" "49caef80cec01ece4de45aa02bc0c2195026733ab72b69c196f06cc47649d776"} {"question" "q-134609" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0.731"] ["math" "0.734"] ["math" "0.736"] ["math" "0.739"] ["math" "0.741"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060524/state.json" "file_sha256" "49caef80cec01ece4de45aa02bc0c2195026733ab72b69c196f06cc47649d776"} {"question" "q-134609" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.736"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060524/state.json" "file_sha256" "49caef80cec01ece4de45aa02bc0c2195026733ab72b69c196f06cc47649d776"} {"question" "q-178291" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the first four terms of the binomial expansion of $(1+x)^{7}$ in ascending powers of $x$ to approximate $(0.96)^{7}.$ Give your answer to five decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060524/state.json" "file_sha256" "49caef80cec01ece4de45aa02bc0c2195026733ab72b69c196f06cc47649d776"} {"question" "q-178291" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that when $n$ is a positive integer, \n\n$$\n(1+x)^{n}=1+nx+\\frac{1}{2!}n(n-1){x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3!}n(n-1)(n-2){x}^{3}+⋯+{x}^{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(1+x)}^{7} & =1+7x+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 7\\cdot 6\\cdot {x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 7\\cdot 6\\cdot 5\\cdot {x}^{3}+⋯+{x}^{7} \\\\  & =1+7x+21{x}^{2}+35{x}^{3}+⋯\\,+{x}^{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(1+x)}^{7}≈1+7x+21{x}^{2}+35{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=-0.04$ into the above approximation of $(1+x)^{7}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0.96)^{7} & =(1+(-0.04))^{7} \\\\  & ≈1+7(-0.04)+21(-0.04)^{2}+35(-0.04)^{3} \\\\  & =1-7(0.04)+21(0.001\\,6)-35(0.000\\,064) \\\\  & =1-0.28+0.033\\,6-0.002\\,24 \\\\  & =0.751\\,36,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto five decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060524/state.json" "file_sha256" "49caef80cec01ece4de45aa02bc0c2195026733ab72b69c196f06cc47649d776"} {"question" "q-178291" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060524/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "657a1d0c9fc298b0bfd76bf9bfcf42171f33a0ba2583e289c6034e93f1c4b415"} {"question" "q-178291" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060