\\  & =\\frac{|(2.1)^{5}-40.8|}{(2.1)^{5}}\\times 100 \\\\  & ≈0.10%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060524/state.json" "file_sha256" "49caef80cec01ece4de45aa02bc0c2195026733ab72b69c196f06cc47649d776"} {"question" "q-134533" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060524/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "657a1d0c9fc298b0bfd76bf9bfcf42171f33a0ba2583e289c6034e93f1c4b415"} {"question" "q-134533" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060524/state.json" "file_sha256" "49caef80cec01ece4de45aa02bc0c2195026733ab72b69c196f06cc47649d776"} {"question" "q-134533" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0.07%"] ["math" "0.08%"] ["math" "0.10%"] ["math" "0.12%"] ["math" "0.15%"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060524/state.json" "file_sha256" "49caef80cec01ece4de45aa02bc0c2195026733ab72b69c196f06cc47649d776"} {"question" "q-134533" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.10%"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060524/state.json" "file_sha256" "49caef80cec01ece4de45aa02bc0c2195026733ab72b69c196f06cc47649d776"} {"question" "e-13020" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the first four terms of the binomial expansion of $(1+x)^6$ in ascending powers of $x$ to approximate $(1.15)^6.$ Give your answer to three decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060524/example-13020.json" "file_sha256" "2c119bec7a7dbe0b4c63e778e8e62ee0da57049754d28d3335ceb9961b8bc90a"} {"question" "e-13020" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that when $n$ is a positive integer, \n\n$$\n(1+x)^n = 1 + nx + \\dfrac{1}{2!} n(n-1)x^2 + \\dfrac{1}{3!} n(n-1)(n-2)x^3 + \\cdots + x^n.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(1+x)}^{6} & =1+6x+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 6\\cdot 5\\cdot {x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 6\\cdot 5\\cdot 4\\cdot {x}^{3}+⋯+{x}^{n} \\\\  & =1+6x+15{x}^{2}+20{x}^{3}+⋯\\,+{x}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\left( {1 + x} \\right)^6 \\approx 1 + 6x + 15x^2 + 20x^3.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=0.15$ into the above approximation of $(1 + x)^6{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1.15)^{6} & =(1+0.15)^{6} \\\\  & ≈1+6(0.15)^{1}+15(0.15)^{2}+20(0.15)^{3} \\\\  & =1+6(0.15)+15(0.022\\,5)+20(0.003\\,375) \\\\  & =1+0.9+0.337\\,5+0.067\\,5 \\\\  & =2.305,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto three decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060524/example-13020.json" "file_sha256" "2c119bec7a7dbe0b4c63e778e8e62ee0da57049754d28d3335ceb9961b8bc90a"} {"question" "e-13021" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the first four terms of the binomial expansion of $(1+x)^8$ in ascending powers of $x$ to approximate $(0.89)^8.$ Give your answer to six decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060524/example-13021.json" "file_sha256" "2e6a2382d30963667372bfbb534baa194385c2c245d48ec918e7a342ea03cd35"} {"question" "e-13021" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that when $n$ is a positive integer, \n\n$$\n(1+x)^n = 1 + nx + \\dfrac{1}{2!} n(n-1)x^2 + \\dfrac{1}{3!} n(n-1)(n-2)x^3 + \\cdots + x^n.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(1+x)}^{8} & =1+8x+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 8\\cdot 7\\cdot {x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 8\\cdot 7\\cdot 6\\cdot {x}^{3}+⋯+{x}^{n} \\\\  & =1+8x+28{x}^{2}+56{x}^{3}+⋯\\,+{x}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\left( {1 + x} \\right)^8 \\approx 1 + 8x + 28x^2 + 56x^3.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=-0.11$ into the above approximation of $(1 + x)^8{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0.89)^{8} & =(1+(-0.11))^{8} \\\\  & ≈1+8(-0.11)^{1}+28(-0.11)^{2}+56(-0.11)^{3} \\\\  & =1-8(0.11)+28(0.012\\,1)-56(0.001\\,331) \\\\  & =1-0.88+0.338\\,8-0.074\\,536 \\\\  & =0.384\\,264,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto six decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060524/example-13021.json" "file_sha256" "2e6a2382d30963667372bfbb534baa194385c2c245d48ec918e7a342ea03cd35"} {"question" "e-13022" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the first $3$ terms of the binomial expansion of ${\\left( 2+x \\right)^{4}}$ in ascending powers of $x$ to approximate $(1.97)^{4}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060524/example-13022.json" "file_sha256" "2b5233b1685ac4ab16c45a9612ff25b3885d3a84bcd30e4610d8d729da6814d7"} {"question" "e-13022" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The binomial theorem for positive integer $n$ states that\n\n \n\n$$\n{(a + b)^n} = {n \\choose 0}{a^n}{b^0} + {n \\choose 1}{a^{n - 1}}{b^1} + {n \\choose 2}{a^{n - 2}}{b^2} +  \\cdots  + {n \\choose n}{a^0}{b^n}.\n$$\n\nBy substituting $a=2,$ $b=x,$ and $n=4,$ we obtain\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(2+x)}^{4}= & (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{0}){(2)}^{4}{(x)}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{1}){(2)}^{4-1}{(x)}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{2}){(2)}^{4-2}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1\\cdot {(2)}^{4}{(x)}^{0}+4{(2)}^{3}{(x)}^{1}+6{(2)}^{2}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =16+4(8)(x)+6(4)({x}^{2})+⋯ \\\\  & =16+32x+24{x}^{2}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\left( {2 + x} \\right)^{4} \\approx 16 + 32x + 24{x^2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=-0.03$ into the expansion of $(2+x)^4{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1.97)^{4} & =(2-0.03)^{4} \\\\  & ≈16+32(-0.03)+24(-0.03)^{2} \\\\  & =16-0.96+0.021\\,6 \\\\  & =15.0616\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060524/example-13022.json" "file_sha256" "2b5233b1685ac4ab16c45a9612ff25b3885d3a84bcd30e4610d8d729da6814d7"} {"question" "e-13023" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate the percentage error obtained when approximating the value of $(1.08)^{5}$ using the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(1+x)^5$ in ascending powers of $x.$ Round your final percentage error to two decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060524/example-13023.json" "file_sha256" "e8fcce15c00bb05e4d87eaab9e805923fed66bb02f2b049e5f46824297fbd0b8"} {"question" "e-13023" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The binomial theorem for positive integer $n$ states that\n\n$$\n{(a + b)^n} = {n \\choose 0}{a^n}{b^0} + {n \\choose 1}{a^{n - 1}}{b^1} + {n \\choose 2}{a^{n - 2}}{b^2} +  \\ldots  + {n \\choose n}{a^0}{b^n}.\n$$\n\nBy substituting $a = 1,$ $b = x,$ and $n = 5,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(1+x)}^{5} & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{0}){(1)}^{5}{(x)}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1}){(1)}^{5-1}{(x)}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{2}){(1)}^{5-2}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1{(1)}^{5}+5{(1)}^{4}{(x)}^{1}+10{(1)}^{3}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1+5(1)(x)+10(1)({x}^{2})+⋯ \\\\  & =1+5x+10{x}^{2}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\left( {1 + x} \\right)^{5}} \\approx 1 + 5x + 10x^2.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $x = 0.08$ into the above approximation of $(1 + x)^5{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1.08)^{5} & =(1+0.08)^{5} \\\\  & ≈1+5(0.08)+10(0.08)^{2} \\\\  & =1+5(0.08)+10(0.006\\,4) \\\\  & =1+0.4+0.064 \\\\  & =1.464\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using a calculator, we can compute the percentage error, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}%\\,error & =\\frac{\\text{amount of error}}{\\text{exact value}}\\times 100 \\\\  & =\\frac{|(1.08)^{5}-1.464|}{(1.08)^{5}}\\times 100 \\\\  & ≈0.36%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060524/example-13023.json" "file_sha256" "e8fcce15c00bb05e4d87eaab9e805923fed66bb02f2b049e5f46824297fbd0b8"}] "usage" {"e-13020" [] "q-18703" [] "e-13022" [] "q-134630" [] "e-13023" [] "e-13021" [] "q-235253" []} "basis" 1969 "retractions" [[17592186101035 :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nUse the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(4 + x)^{4}$ in ascending powers of $x$ to approximate $(4.05)^{4}$. Give your answer to three decimal places."] [17592186101243 :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nUse the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(5 + x)^{7}$ in ascending powers of $x$ to approximate $(4.97)^{7}$. Round your answer to two decimal places.$(4.97)^{7} \\approx$\n{{field-1}}\n\n---\n\n<a id=\"calculating-the-percentage-error-of-an-approximation\"></a>"] [17592186101202 :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nCalculate the percentage error obtained when approximating the value of $(3.2)^{5}$ using the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(3 + x)^{5}$ in ascending powers of $x$. Round your final percentage error to one decimal place."] [17592186100509 :question/problem "Use the first four terms of the binomial expansion of $(1+x)^6$ in ascending powers of $x$ to approximate $(1.15)^6$. Give your answer to three decimal places."] [17592186100509 :question/worked-solution "Recall that when $n$ is a positive integer,\n\n$$\n(1+x)^n = 1 + nx + \\dfrac{1}{2!} n(n-1)x^2 + \\dfrac{1}{3!} n(n-1)(n-2)x^3 + \\cdots + x^n\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(1 + x)^{6} &= 1 + 6x + \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 6 \\cdot 5 \\cdot x^{2} + \\frac{1}{6} \\cdot 6 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 4 \\cdot x^{3} + ⋯ + x^{n} \\\\\n&= 1 + 6x + 15x^{2} + 20x^{3} + ⋯ + x^{n}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x$, we have\n\n$$\n\\left({1 + x} \\right)^6 \\approx 1 + 6x + 15x^2 + 20x^3\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=0.15$ into the above approximation of $(1 + x)^6{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(1.15)^{6} &= (1 + 0.15)^{6} \\\\\n&\\approx 1 + 6(0.15)^{1} + 15(0.15)^{2} + 20(0.15)^{3} \\\\\n&= 1 + 6(0.15) + 15(0.0225) + 20(0.003375) \\\\\n&= 1 + 0.9 + 0.3375 + 0.0675 \\\\\n&= 2.305\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto three decimal places."] [17592186100510 :question/problem "Use the first four terms of the binomial expansion of $(1+x)^8$ in ascending powers of $x$ to approximate $(0.89)^8$. Give your answer to six decimal places."] [17592186100510 :question/worked-solution "Recall that when $n$ is a positive integer,\n\n$$\n(1+x)^n = 1 + nx + \\dfrac{1}{2!} n(n-1)x^2 + \\dfrac{1}{3!} n(n-1)(n-2)x^3 + \\cdots + x^n\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(1 + x)^{8} &= 1 + 8x + \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 8 \\cdot 7 \\cdot x^{2} + \\frac{1}{6} \\cdot 8 \\cdot 7 \\cdot 6 \\cdot x^{3} + ⋯ + x^{n} \\\\\n&= 1 + 8x + 28x^{2} + 56x^{3} + ⋯ + x^{n}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x$, we have\n\n$$\n\\left({1 + x} \\right)^8 \\approx 1 + 8x + 28x^2 + 56x^3\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=-0.11$ into the above approximation of $(1 + x)^8{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(0.89)^{8} &= (1 + (-0.11))^{8} \\\\\n&\\approx 1 + 8(-0.11)^{1} + 28(-0.11)^{2} + 56(-0.11)^{3} \\\\\n&= 1 - 8(0.11) + 28(0.0121) - 56(0.001331) \\\\\n&= 1 - 0.88 + 0.3388 - 0.074536 \\\\\n&= 0.384264\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto six decimal places."] [17592186100511 :question/problem "Use the first $3$ terms of the binomial expansion of ${\\left(2+x \\right)^{4}}$ in ascending powers of $x$ to approximate $(1.97)^{4}$."] [17592186100511 :question/worked-solution "The binomial theorem for positive integer $n$ states that\n\n$$\n{(a + b)^n} = {n \\choose 0}{a^n}{b^0} + {n \\choose 1}{a^{n - 1}}{b^1} + {n \\choose 2}{a^{n - 2}}{b^2} + \\cdots + {n \\choose n}{a^0}{b^n}\n$$\n\nBy substituting $a=2$, $b=x$, and $n=4$, we obtain\n\n$$\n(2 + x)^{4} = \\mid (\\frac{4}{0})(2)^{4}(x)^{0} + (\\frac{4}{1})(2)^{4 - 1}(x)^{1} + (\\frac{4}{2})(2)^{4 - 2}(x)^{2} + ⋯; = 1 \\cdot (2)^{4}(x)^{0} + 4(2)^{3}(x)^{1} + 6(2)^{2}(x)^{2} + ⋯; = 16 + 4(8)(x) + 6(4)(x^{2}) + ⋯; = 16 + 32x + 24x^{2} + ⋯\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x$, we have\n\n$$\n\\left({2 + x} \\right)^{4} \\approx 16 + 32x + 24{x^2}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=-0.03$ into the expansion of $(2+x)^4{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(1.97)^{4} &= (2 - 0.03)^{4} \\\\\n&\\approx 16 + 32(-0.03) + 24(-0.03)^{2} \\\\\n&= 16 - 0.96 + 0.0216 \\\\\n&= 15.0616\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186100512 :question/problem "Calculate the percentage error obtained when approximating the value of $(1.08)^{5}$ using the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(1+x)^5$ in ascending powers of $x$. Round your final percentage error to two decimal places."] [17592186100512 :question/worked-solution "The binomial theorem for positive integer $n$ states that\n\n$$\n{(a + b)^n} = {n \\choose 0}{a^n}{b^0} + {n \\choose 1}{a^{n - 1}}{b^1} + {n \\choose 2}{a^{n - 2}}{b^2} + \\ldots + {n \\choose n}{a^0}{b^n}\n$$\n\nBy substituting $a = 1$, $b = x$, and $n = 5$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(1 + x)^{5} &= (\\frac{5}{0})(1)^{5}(x)^{0} + (\\frac{5}{1})(1)^{5 - 1}(x)^{1} + (\\frac{5}{2})(1)^{5 - 2}(x)^{2} + ⋯ \\\\\n&= 1(1)^{5} + 5(1)^{4}(x)^{1} + 10(1)^{3}(x)^{2} + ⋯ \\\\\n&= 1 + 5(1)(x) + 10(1)(x^{2}) + ⋯ \\\\\n&= 1 + 5x + 10x^{2} + ⋯\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x$, we have\n\n$$\n{\\left({1 + x} \\right)^{5}} \\approx 1 + 5x + 10x^2\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $x = 0.08$ into the above approximation of $(1 + x)^5{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(1.08)^{5} &= (1 + 0.08)^{5} \\\\\n&\\approx 1 + 5(0.08) + 10(0.08)^{2} \\\\\n&= 1 + 5(0.08) + 10(0.0064) \\\\\n&= 1 + 0.4 + 0.064 \\\\\n&= 1.464\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using a calculator, we can compute the percentage error, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n%error &= \\frac{amount of error}{exact value} \\cdot 100 \\\\\n&= (\\mid (1.08)^{5} - 1.464 \\mid)/((1.08)^{5}) \\cdot 100 \\\\\n&\\approx 0.36%\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186097981 17592186097982 17592186097983 17592186097984 17592186097985 17592186098969 17592186098970 17592186098971 17592186098972 17592186098973 17592186232850] "immutable_fields" [[17592186097980 :answer-field/correct] [17592186097980 :answer-field/id] [17592186097980 :answer-field/key] [17592186097980 :answer-field/type] [17592186098968 :answer-field/correct] [17592186098968 :answer-field/id] [17592186098968 :answer-field/key] [17592186098968 :answer-field/type] [17592186099185 :answer-field/correct] [17592186099185 :answer-field/id] [17592186099185 :answer-field/key] [17592186099185 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
